Step-by-step: Build the BizOS APK
Step 1 — Install Java JDK 17
Capacitor's Android build requires Java 17.

Go to https://adoptium.net
Download Temurin 17 (LTS) → Windows x64 .msi installer
Run the installer — on the install options screen, enable "Set JAVA_HOME variable"
Verify: open a new terminal and run:

java -version
Should print openjdk 17.x.x

Step 2 — Install Android Studio
Go to https://developer.android.com/studio
Download and run the installer, keep all defaults
On first launch, the Setup Wizard runs — click through it, it will download the Android SDK automatically (this takes a few minutes)
Once done, you'll land on the Android Studio welcome screen — that's all you need for now

Step 3 — Set your backend URL
Open bizos-frontend/.env.local and make sure NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL points to your real server — not localhost:


NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://your-actual-backend.com/api/v1
If the backend is running on your local machine and you just want to test, use your machine's local IP address instead of localhost (Android can't reach your PC's localhost):


NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.x.x:8000/api/v1

Step 4 — Build the app
Open a terminal in bizos-frontend/ and run:


npm run build:android
This does two things:

next build — compiles the app into the out/ folder
npx cap sync android — copies out/ into the Android project and syncs plugins
Wait for both to finish with no errors.

Step 5 — Open in Android Studio

npm run open:android
Android Studio opens the android/ project. Wait for it to finish Gradle sync (progress bar at the bottom) — this can take 2–5 minutes the first time.

Step 6 — Build the APK
In Android Studio:

Top menu → Build → Build Bundle(s) / APK(s) → Build APK(s)
Wait for the build to finish (progress bar at the bottom)
A popup appears: "APK(s) generated successfully" → click "locate"
The APK is at:


bizos-frontend/android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
Step 7 — Install on your phone
Option A — USB cable:

On your Android phone: Settings → About Phone → tap Build Number 7 times to enable Developer Mode
Settings → Developer Options → enable USB Debugging
Plug in via USB → your phone appears in Android Studio's device list
Click the Run button (▶) in Android Studio — it installs and launches directly
Option B — Share the file:

Copy app-debug.apk to your phone (WhatsApp, Google Drive, USB, etc.)
Open the file on your phone → tap Install
If prompted about "Install unknown apps" → allow it for the app you used to transfer the file
Every time you update the app
Just run this one command and then rebuild in Android Studio:


npm run build:android
Then in Android Studio: Build → Build APK(s) again.